    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I'm looking for different ways to bake bread at home. Can you list some methods?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are ways for baking bread at home?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "here are popular home - bread methods you can try : - straight - dough ( yeasted ) : mix, knead, proof, bake. fast and reliable. - preferments : poolish, biga, or pate fermentee ( old dough ) added to a yeasted dough for deeper flavor and better crumb. - sourdough ( natural starter ) : longer, slower fermentation ; most complex flavor and great crust. - no - knead artisan loaf : high hydration, minimal handling, long ferment ; bake in a preheated dutch oven. - \u201c artisan bread in five minutes a day \u201d : mix once, refrigerate dough up to 2 weeks ; pull off portions and bake as needed. - ciabatta - style : very wet dough with gentle shaping for open crumb and crisp crust. - enriched pan breads : sandwich loaves, brioche, challah \u2014 baked in loaf pans. - whole - grain / seeded loaves : higher nutrition ; often benefit from preferments or soakers. - cold retard ( any method ) : overnight fridge proofing for flavor and easier scheduling. baking setups : - dutch oven",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0020-07-10247:0": -0.5888758301734924,
            "clueweb22-en0042-43-01945:16": -0.66717928647995,
            "clueweb22-en0013-76-05236:7": -0.9264943599700928,
            "clueweb22-en0033-16-11038:5": -0.9394732713699341,
            "clueweb22-en0042-42-17701:4": -0.9416166543960571
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Nice, thanks! What would you say are the advantages of baking bread in my own oven at home?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are advantages of baking bread in my own oven at home?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "big advantages to baking at home : - control and health : choose simple ingredients ( flour, water, salt, yeast ) and adjust salt, sugar, whole grains, and seeds to your needs and your child \u2019 s. - freshness and flavor : nothing beats just - baked aroma and a crisp crust with a tender crumb. - customization : fold in your garden herbs, cheeses, nuts, or even a touch of your favorite hot sauces. - cost and convenience : inexpensive staples ; bake on your schedule. dough can be cold - proofed overnight for easy timing. - quality with simple gear : a preheated dutch oven traps steam and provides steady heat, boosting oven spring, color, and shine while simplifying the process. - consistency : the pot \u2019 s thermal mass evens out temperature swings for reliable results in a home oven. - variety : from lean sourdough to enriched sandwich loaves, baguettes, ciabatta, and no \u2011 knead \u2014 one oven, many styles. - family activity : kid - friendly shaping and scoring ; great for sharing with guests. - skill - building : satisfying craft that rewards practice and experimentation. - better than store - bought shelf life : no additive",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0004-88-01683:0": -1.556922435760498,
            "clueweb22-en0029-91-02579:2": -1.887010931968689,
            "clueweb22-en0025-19-14992:4": -3.075085401535034,
            "clueweb22-en0034-33-08941:3": -3.488029718399048,
            "clueweb22-en0034-12-18872:5": -3.8323163986206055
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That all sounds great. What\u2019s a simple, reliable bread recipe I should try first if I want to get that fresh, crisp crust?",
          "user_rubrik": "What is a good recipe for baking bread in the oven at home?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "try this no \u2011 knead dutch \u2011 oven loaf \u2014 super reliable with a crackly crust. ingredients - 400 g bread or all \u2011 purpose flour ( about 3 1 / 4 cups ) - 1 / 2 tsp instant yeast - 1 1 / 2 tsp fine salt - 300 ml lukewarm water ( about 1 1 / 4 cups ) method 1 ) mix flour, yeast, and salt. add water ; stir until a shaggy dough forms. cover and rest 12 \u2013 18 hours at room temp. 2 ) generously flour the counter ; tip out the sticky dough. with floured hands, shape into a tight ball. 3 ) set on parchment or a silicone bread sling ; cover 30 minutes. meanwhile, place a heavy lidded pot in the oven and preheat to 230 c / 450 f. 4 ) lower dough into the hot pot, cover, and bake 30 minutes. uncover and bake 15 \u2013 20 minutes more, until deep golden and crisp ( about 96 c / 205 f internal ). 5 ) cool on a rack at least 45 minutes before slicing. extras - for even crisper crust, chill the shaped loaf 30 \u2013 60 minutes before baking. - add rosemary or seeds from your garden ; score just",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0042-98-05537:3": -2.3762550354003906,
            "clueweb22-en0042-98-05537:4": -3.0384654998779297,
            "clueweb22-en0010-48-18907:1": -4.154980659484863,
            "clueweb22-en0033-89-01269:1": -4.563498497009277,
            "clueweb22-en0033-51-19484:1": -4.7752180099487305
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Looks perfect! If I have fatty liver, would it be better to use whole wheat flour or stick with white flour for this kind of bread?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have fatty liver. How does that change which breads are healthy when baking at home in the oven?\u00a0",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Whole grain beats white for fatty liver. More fiber and a lower glycemic impact support better blood sugar/insulin control, which is helpful for liver health. Aim for at least a blend.  Easy tweaks to your no\u2011knead loaf: - 50% whole wheat: 200 g whole wheat + 200 g bread/AP flour; water 320\u2013340 ml (dough should be sticky). Same yeast/salt and method. - Add rye for extra fiber: 80\u2013150 g rye flour + the rest whole wheat/white; increase water by 20\u201340 ml. Rye tastes stronger; blending keeps it family\u2011friendly. - 100% whole wheat: 400 g whole wheat; water 340\u2013360 ml. Expect a tighter crumb but great flavor.  Tips - Whole grains absorb more water; err wetter for a crisp crust. - An overnight ferment (as in your recipe) helps flavor and digestibility. - Boost fiber with 20\u201330 g oats or seeds. - Keep portions moderate; pair with salads, lean proteins, or olive\u2011oil\u2013based spreads.  If you enjoy it, a sourdough version of the same loaf can be even gentler on post\u2011meal glucose.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0013-69-14135:4": -3.711524248123169,
            "clueweb22-en0033-96-02903:4": -3.711524248123169,
            "clueweb22-en0033-96-02903:15": -3.935211658477783,
            "clueweb22-en0012-29-17750:0": -3.9532382488250732,
            "clueweb22-en0046-25-14652:5": -4.002344131469727
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great tips, thanks! Do I need to change anything about baking time or temperature if I use more whole grains?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have some basic tools for baking bread in the oven at home. What else do I need to get for baking bread in the oven?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Short answer: usually keep the same temperature; time may run a bit longer.  - Temp: Stick with 450\u00b0F/230\u00b0C and a fully preheated Dutch oven. - Time: Whole grains (especially 100% whole wheat or rye blends) often need 5\u201310 minutes more total bake to dry the crumb.   - Try: 30\u201335 minutes covered, then 15\u201320 minutes uncovered. - Doneness cues:   - Internal temperature 205\u00b0F/96\u00b0C (up to 210\u00b0F/99\u00b0C for very whole\u2011grain loaves).   - Deep golden-brown crust; loaf sounds hollow; sides feel firm, not squishy. - If the crust darkens too fast before the center is done:   - Keep the lid on 5 extra minutes, or   - Drop the oven to 430\u2013440\u00b0F (220\u2013225\u00b0C) for the last part, or   - Finish the final 5 minutes out of the pot directly on the rack. - Let it cool 45\u201360 minutes to set the crumb (whole grains hold more moisture).",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0011-54-10606:13": 0.687982439994812,
            "clueweb22-en0035-95-05538:15": 0.5306423306465149,
            "clueweb22-en0023-92-03552:6": 0.405191034078598,
            "clueweb22-en0018-34-08615:14": 0.2430630922317505,
            "clueweb22-en0008-01-00349:13": 0.19502893090248108
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Super clear, cheers! My tools are pretty basic\u2014just a regular oven, mixing bowls, baking tray, and a basic scale. What else should I get to bake bread in the oven?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have some basic tools for baking bread in the oven at home. What else do I need to get for baking bread in the oven?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "great starting kit already. here \u2019 s what i \u2019 d add, prioritized for crisp, reliable loaves : essential upgrades - 4 \u2013 6 l cast - iron dutch oven with lid ( traps steam for bakery crust ) - parchment paper or a silicone bread sling ( safe loading / unloading ) - instant \u2011 read thermometer ( check 96 \u2013 99\u00b0c / 205 \u2013 210\u00b0f internal doneness ) - bench scraper + flexible dough scraper ( clean handling, easy shaping ) - oven thermometer ( home ovens run hot / cold ) for proofing and shaping - banneton basket ( s ) with liner ( or a colander lined with a floured towel ) - 6 \u2013 8 l clear lidded container for bulk fermentation ( watch dough rise ) for scoring and steam - bread lame or single \u2011 edge razor ( controlled expansion ) - if not using a dutch oven : baking steel / stone + a metal bowl or roasting pan to cover the loaf for the first 20 \u2013 25 min ; spray bottle for misting for specific styles - loaf pan or pullman pan ( sandwich bread ) - perforated baguette pan + linen couche ( baguettes ) nice to have - cooling",
          "citations": {
