    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Looks perfect! If I have fatty liver, would it be better to use whole wheat flour or stick with white flour for this kind of bread?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have fatty liver. How does that change which breads are healthy when baking at home in the oven?\u00a0",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Whole grain beats white for fatty liver. More fiber and a lower glycemic impact support better blood sugar/insulin control, which is helpful for liver health. Aim for at least a blend.  Easy tweaks to your no\u2011knead loaf: - 50% whole wheat: 200 g whole wheat + 200 g bread/AP flour; water 320\u2013340 ml (dough should be sticky). Same yeast/salt and method. - Add rye for extra fiber: 80\u2013150 g rye flour + the rest whole wheat/white; increase water by 20\u201340 ml. Rye tastes stronger; blending keeps it family\u2011friendly. - 100% whole wheat: 400 g whole wheat; water 340\u2013360 ml. Expect a tighter crumb but great flavor.  Tips - Whole grains absorb more water; err wetter for a crisp crust. - An overnight ferment (as in your recipe) helps flavor and digestibility. - Boost fiber with 20\u201330 g oats or seeds. - Keep portions moderate; pair with salads, lean proteins, or olive\u2011oil\u2013based spreads.  If you enjoy it, a sourdough version of the same loaf can be even gentler on post\u2011meal glucose.",
          "citations": {
