    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great tips, thanks! Do I need to change anything about baking time or temperature if I use more whole grains?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have some basic tools for baking bread in the oven at home. What else do I need to get for baking bread in the oven?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Short answer: usually keep the same temperature; time may run a bit longer.  - Temp: Stick with 450\u00b0F/230\u00b0C and a fully preheated Dutch oven. - Time: Whole grains (especially 100% whole wheat or rye blends) often need 5\u201310 minutes more total bake to dry the crumb.   - Try: 30\u201335 minutes covered, then 15\u201320 minutes uncovered. - Doneness cues:   - Internal temperature 205\u00b0F/96\u00b0C (up to 210\u00b0F/99\u00b0C for very whole\u2011grain loaves).   - Deep golden-brown crust; loaf sounds hollow; sides feel firm, not squishy. - If the crust darkens too fast before the center is done:   - Keep the lid on 5 extra minutes, or   - Drop the oven to 430\u2013440\u00b0F (220\u2013225\u00b0C) for the last part, or   - Finish the final 5 minutes out of the pot directly on the rack. - Let it cool 45\u201360 minutes to set the crumb (whole grains hold more moisture).",
          "citations": {
