    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I\u2019m curious about baking bread at home\u2014what are some different methods?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are ways for baking bread at home?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Great question \u2014 there are several common home-bread methods:  - Hand-kneading: traditional control of texture and crumb; good for most yeasted recipes.   - Stand mixer or bread machine: saves labor (machine does the kneading); bread machines leave a paddle in the loaf so many remove dough to finish in the oven.   - No\u2011knead (traditional): very little handling, long bulk fermentation (8\u201324 hrs) develops flavor; bake in a covered pot/Dutch oven for good crust.   - No\u2011knead \u201cturbo\u201d: same principle but sped up (about 2\u00bd hours) for quicker results.   - Hands\u2011free/spoon technique: mix with a spoon or use a spoon-handle to manipulate dough\u2014minimal equipment.   - Sourdough: uses starter (levain) and often long, cool ferments and multiple folds instead of intensive kneading.  Also consider baking vessel: bread pan, Dutch oven (for boules), long covered bakers or \u201cpoor man\u2019s\u201d Dutch oven; each affects crust and shape. Basic ingredients are typically flour, water, salt and yeast (starter for sourdough).",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0046-36-14432:1": 53.90079879760742,
            "clueweb22-en0033-09-13131:1": 50.714298248291016,
            "clueweb22-en0046-36-14432:2": 49.979698181152344,
            "clueweb22-en0040-34-12210:0": 49.04349899291992,
            "clueweb22-en0040-32-15020:2": 47.9109001159668
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That's helpful, thanks! What are some advantages of baking bread in my own oven at home, though?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are advantages of baking bread in my own oven at home?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Great question \u2014 home ovens give you a lot of useful advantages:  - Superior crust and oven spring: you can reach higher initial heat, and by trapping steam (e.g., baking in a Dutch oven or using a covered baker) you get that crisp, thick crust and better rise.   - Control over texture and size: ovens let you bake larger or shaped loaves (boules, b\u00e2tards, sandwich loaves) and adjust temperatures/times for different crumb structures.   - Versatility: you can bake sourdough, enriched breads, pizzas, and use different vessels (Dutch oven, baking stone/steel, loaf pans).   - Even baking: with proper preheating and tools (stones/steels) you get a consistent, professional-like bake.   - No special equipment required: regular flour and basic tools are fine; you don\u2019t need a dedicated machine.    Quick tips: preheat thoroughly, use a baking stone or Dutch oven to retain heat, and trap steam during the first part of the bake for best results.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0025-19-14992:0": 18.791200637817383,
            "clueweb22-en0004-80-08623:5": 17.27669906616211,
            "clueweb22-en0028-08-10427:3": 17.15880012512207,
            "clueweb22-en0024-59-06962:2": 16.896400451660156,
            "clueweb22-en0028-08-10427:4": 16.62849998474121
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense! Could you suggest a really simple bread recipe I can try this weekend? Something I can bake in a Dutch oven would be ideal.",
          "user_rubrik": "What is a good recipe for baking bread in the oven at home?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Simple no\u2011knead Dutch\u2011oven boule  Ingredients - 500 g (about 4 cups) all\u2011purpose flour - 375 g (75% hydration) water, room temp (~1 2/3 cups) - 10 g (2 tsp) salt - 1/4 tsp instant yeast (\u22481 g)  Method 1. In a large bowl stir flour, water, yeast and salt until a shaggy, sticky dough forms. Cover and leave at room temp 12\u201318 hours (overnight) until doubled and bubbly. 2. Turn dough onto a lightly floured surface, fold a couple times to shape into a round. Place seam\u2011side down on parchment, cover and proof 1\u20132 hours. 3. 30\u201345 min before baking preheat oven to 450\u00b0F (230\u00b0C) with a lidded Dutch oven inside. 4. Score the dough, lift parchment with dough into the hot pot, cover and bake 30 minutes. 5. Remove lid and bake 10\u201320 more minutes until deep golden and crusty. 6. Cool on a rack at least 1 hour before slicing.  Tips: use parchment to transfer, and let loaf cool fully for best crumb.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0021-71-13340:3": 42.632999420166016,
            "clueweb22-en0023-64-15809:12": 42.34230041503906,
            "clueweb22-en0012-98-00263:3": 42.221099853515625,
            "clueweb22-en0037-96-09606:5": 41.93899917602539,
            "clueweb22-en0017-56-13642:10": 41.9297981262207
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Perfect, thanks! One more thing: I have fatty liver. How does that change which breads are healthy when baking at home in the oven?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have fatty liver. How does that change which breads are healthy when baking at home in the oven?\u00a0",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "good question \u2014 with fatty liver you \u2019 ll want to favor breads that slow glucose absorption and add fiber, and avoid refined, sugary loaves. practical guidelines for home baking - use whole \u2011 grain or sprouted flours ( sprouted grain flour has more fiber, antioxidants and amino acids than refined flour ). - avoid recipes that rely on enriched / bleached white flour or add sugars ( keep added sugar to zero ). - boost fiber and healthy fats : add rolled oats, flax or chia seeds, or a portion of whole \u2011 grain rye / spelt. - target at least ~ 2 g fiber per serving and keep slices modest in size ; avoid very high \u2011 calorie, high \u2011 sugar inclusions ( dried fruit, sweeteners ). - prefer long, cool ferments ( no \u2011 knead or sourdough styles ) \u2014 they improve flavor and texture so you won \u2019 t need sugar / fats to compensate. - don \u2019 t trust labels like \u201c wheat \u201d alone ; use true whole \u2011 grain or sprouted flour in your recipe. these choices help reduce big blood \u2011 sugar spikes and support weight and liver health while still letting you enjoy home \u2011 baked bread",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0034-02-17207:12": 59.4463005065918,
            "clueweb22-en0024-63-14863:5": 55.08829879760742,
            "clueweb22-en0034-02-17207:11": 53.24869918823242,
            "clueweb22-en0024-63-14863:4": 53.21989822387695,
            "clueweb22-en0042-38-09475:0": 52.84049987792969
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That's really useful, thanks! I like including seeds\u2014do you have any tips for adding them to basic breads?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have some basic tools for baking bread in the oven at home. What else do I need to get for baking bread in the oven?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "nice \u2014 seeds are a great way to add fiber, texture and healthy fats. practical tips : - soak most seeds : sunflower, poppy, sesame, flax, etc. soak in cold water at least equal to seed weight ( i like overnight / ~ 12 hours ). add the whole soaker ( seeds + soaking water ) to the dough when you mix or before the first set of stretch \u2011 and \u2011 folds so you don \u2019 t rob the dough of hydration. - adjust dough hydration : increase total water a few percentage points when adding soaked seeds / soakers or lots of dry seeds. - flax and chia options : grind flax for better nutrient access, or soak flax / chia to form a gel ( chia ~ 1 : 16 chia : water for a drink ; flax / chia gels also act as binders in doughs ). camelina can also be soaked overnight or used raw. - when to incorporate : add seeds during initial mix or early in bulk fermentation ( before folds ) so they distribute evenly without damaging gluten. - texture / nutrition : whole seeds give crunch ; ground or gelled seeds add body and help retain moisture.",
          "citations": {
