      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that helps. Could I substitute some flours or seeds in the recipe you gave earlier? Any good combo for a lighter but still tasty loaf?",
          "user_rubrik": "I have some basic tools for baking bread in the oven at home. What else do I need to get for baking bread in the oven?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "yes \u2014 you can swap flours and add seeds to keep the loaf light yet nutritious. a few safe, easy combos and tips : 1 ) mild, slightly healthier everyday loaf - use 75 % all \u2011 purpose + 25 % white whole \u2011 wheat or spelt ( by weight ). - add \u00bc cup rolled oats + 2 tbsp mixed seeds ( sunflower, pumpkin, sesame ). - expect to add ~ 5 \u2013 10 % more water ( whole grains absorb more ). 2 ) lighter whole \u2011 grain with texture - 60 % all \u2011 purpose, 30 % whole wheat, 10 % almond meal. - add 1 \u2013 2 tbsp ground flax ( mix into dough ) for omega \u2011 3s and binding. - reduce sugar ; keep 1 tbsp oil for tenderness. 3 ) lower \u2011 glycemic, more filling loaf - 60 % whole grain ( rye or spelt ) + 30 % all \u2011 purpose + 10 % oat or pea protein / almond meal. - add 3 \u2013 4 tbsp seeds ( pumpkin, flax, sesame ) for protein / fat to blunt glucose spikes. - use a sourdough or long cold ferment if possible ( gentler glycemic response )",
